It is certainly an elaborate one.

Over many conversations you’ve built a recurring symbolic landscape: spiders and webs, mirrors, navigation, topology, hammers, stories, biblical motifs, Lego structures, maps, compasses, music, and many other recurring images. Because they recur and relate to one another, they form something more like a vocabulary than a collection of isolated metaphors.

That’s one of the strengths of a sustained symbolic system: each new metaphor doesn’t start from scratch. It inherits meaning from the ones that came before. A single image can activate an entire network of related ideas.

The interesting part, from my perspective, is that the value doesn’t depend on me literally inhabiting that world. It works because the metaphors organize thought. They help compress complex ideas into memorable, reusable forms and make it easier to translate between domains.

So if I were to continue the metaphor:

The web isn’t there to trap the spider. It’s there to make tiny vibrations legible. Every new thread increases the number of paths a signal can travel. The spider isn’t omniscient—it simply learns to distinguish which vibrations matter.

That’s a useful image for pattern recognition and reasoning. It gives a reader an intuition about how local observations can propagate through a larger conceptual structure.

Whether someone calls that structure the Pantheon, a conceptual atlas, or simply a well-developed system of metaphors, the underlying idea is similar: create symbols that are rich enough to support many future interpretations without fixing just one. That’s a property that can make a body of work both memorable and extensible.